   "note": "",
   "body": "Could you imagine that—\n\nCrinkled, calloused hands\nworking on a new home for your bride-to-be.\nA girl with no coin from a town with no name,\nso these stone walls you are stacking\nare the only way you know\nto build a hedge against tomorrow.\n\nCould you imagine that—\n\nCrinkled, calloused hands\nholding your tools from the day,\nwalking back to your father’s house,\nlimbs sore and heavy,\nonly to find your betrothed Mary crying.\nTears on her face, but not of terror.\nJoyful tears. A terrifying wonder.\n“Overshadowed by the Spirit,” she whispers,\nand the future you squared with line and cord\ncollapses into rubble at your feet.\n\nCould you imagine that—\n\nCrinkled, calloused hands\nbarely grasping on,\nsitting alone in the dark with the law.\nYou’re a righteous man. This is the only way.\nPlanning a quiet divorce. That’s mercy.\nYour hand-built future slipping out of view,\nuntil those calloused fists slam into stone,\nsplitting your knuckles in the dark.\nYour good name, your family honor,\nwhat little your family has,\nwhittled away before you even speak a word.\n\nCould you imagine that—\n\nCrinkled, calloused hands\ncovered in dust, blood, and tears,\nready to resign.\nBut then a bright light,\nand those hands go up to shield in blindness.\n“Joseph, son of David, do not be afraid\nto take Mary as your wife.\nYou are to name him Jesus.”\nThe terror breaks.\nYour white-knuckled grip turns from fist to palm.\n\nYou wake the next morning with empty hands,\nleaving the blueprint behind,\nchoosing to build a shelter\nfor a story that is no longer yours to steer.\n\nCould you imagine that—"
